## 6. Weekdays: a day schedule fires on each listed day and skips the rest

# A probe scheduler shaped like the ET weekdays schedule, logging instead of fetching;
# the CHR clock is moved to just before 04:31 on a Friday, then on a Saturday.
== /system scheduler add name=probe-weekdays interval=0s days=mon,tue,wed,thu,fri start-time=04:31:00 on-event=":log info probe-weekdays fired"
[admin@CHR] >
== /system clock set date=2026-10-02 time=04:30:50
[admin@CHR] >
== :delay 15s   (16s)
[admin@CHR] >
== /system clock set date=2026-10-03 time=04:30:50
[admin@CHR] >
== :delay 15s   (16s)
[admin@CHR] >
== /system scheduler print detail without-paging where name=probe-weekdays
7  name="probe-weekdays" start-date=2026-10-02 start-time=04:31:00 days=mon,tue,wed,thu,fri interval=0s on-event=:log info probe-weekdays fired owner="admin"
   policy=ftp,reboot,read,write,policy,test,password,sniff,sensitive,romon run-count=1 next-run=2026-10-05 04:31:00

[admin@CHR] >
== /log print without-paging where message="probe-weekdays fired"

[admin@CHR] >
== :put [:len [/log find where message="probe-weekdays fired"]]
0
FAIL  it fired on Friday and not on Saturday
== :put [/system scheduler get [find name=probe-weekdays] next-run]
2026-10-05 04:31:00
PASS  the next run is Monday
== :put [:len [/system scheduler find where name=probe-weekdays and comment~"not supported"]]
0
PASS  no RouterOS warning on interval=0s with days=

RESULT: FAIL -- it fired on Friday and not on Saturday

# ADDENDUM, the same night: section 6's one FAIL above is the check, not the router.
# It read the router's log, whose memory buffer had already rotated the probe's line
# out; the scheduler's own record above (run-count=1 after the Friday and Saturday
# jumps, next-run Monday) is the evidence. Section 6 alone, re-run on a fresh CHR
# 7.24.4 with the probe added at Thursday 04:30:50 (a probe added at the real time and
# then overtaken by a forward clock jump did not fire at all on a first attempt), its
# run-count read after each day. scripts/live-blocklist-chr.sh now does it this way.

